     "page": 1,
     "text": "CLINICAL SUMMARY - Edwards-Burns Clinic\nPatient: Breanna Miller DOB: 10/10/1989 Sex: F MRN: 972061\n\nEncounters:\n 2024-08-23 Type 2 diabetes BP 135/89 Wt 214\n 2024-03-02 Type 2 diabetes BP 134/77 Wt 228\n 2023-09-09 Type 2 diabetes BP 134/88 Wt 222\n 2023-03-18 Type 2 diabetes BP 119/80 Wt 213\n 2022-09-24 Type 2 diabetes BP 129/77 Wt 226\n 2022-04-02 lab review BP 134/87 Wt 215\n\nAssessment/Plan:\n • DM2 (A1c 3.2; duration 6 yrs; dx at age 30) - controlled; meds: metformin 500 mg\n\nCLINICAL ENCOUNTERS:\n\n**2024-08-23**\nMs. Breanna Miller, a 34-year-old female, presented for a follow-up of her Type 2 diabetes. Her blood pressure at this visit was 135/89 mmHg, and her weight was 214 lbs. The patient reported no significant changes in her diet or exercise regimen over the past few months. The physical examination was otherwise unremarkable, with normal findings in the cardiovascular, respiratory, and abdominal systems. Laboratory tests revealed a continued stable A1c level of 3.2%, indicating excellent glycemic control.\n\n**2024-03-02**\nMs. Miller returned for a follow-up on her Type 2 diabetes. Her blood pressure was recorded at 134/77 mmHg, and her weight was 228 lbs. The patient mentioned a minor upper respiratory tract infection two weeks prior, which resolved without complications. The physical exam showed no abnormalities, with a heart rate of 76 bpm and a respiratory rate of 16 breaths per minute. Her A1c level was 3.3%, reflecting stable control over her diabetes.\n\n**2023-09-09**\nIn her visit on September 9, 2023, Ms. Miller’s blood pressure was 134/88 mmHg, and she weighed 222 lbs. She described mild fatigue, which she attributed to a recent bout of seasonal allergies. The remainder of her physical exam was unremarkable. Her A1c level was 3.3%, consistent with well-controlled diabetes. The patient expressed satisfaction with her current metformin regimen.\n\n**2023-03-18**\nOn March 18, 2023, Ms. Miller visited the clinic for a routine check-up of her Type 2 diabetes. Her blood pressure was 119/80 mmHg, and she weighed 213 lbs. The patient reported no significant symptoms, and her physical exam showed no abnormalities. Her A1c level was 3.2%, which is within the target range, indicating effective diabetes management.\n\n**2022-09-24**\nDuring the September 24, 2022, visit, Ms. Miller’s blood pressure was 129/77 mmHg, and her weight was 226 lbs. The patient reported feeling well overall and denied any symptoms of hypoglycemia or hyperglycemia. The physical exam was normal. Her A1c level was 3.2%, reflecting stable blood gluco"
    },
    {
     "page": 2,
     "text": "se control.\n\n**2022-04-02**\nOn April 2, 2022, Ms. Miller attended a follow-up visit for a lab review of her Type 2 diabetes. Her blood pressure was 734/87 mmHg, and she weighed 215 lbs. She reported no new symptoms and denied any recent illnesses. The physical exam was unremarkable. Her A1c level was 3.3%, indicating effective management of her diabetes.\n\n**A1C TREND:**\n- 08/24: 3.2\n- 03/24: 3.3\n- 09/23: 3.3\n- 03/23: 3.2\n\n**SOCIAL HISTORY:**\nMs. Miller has a history of tobacco use, having smoked cigarettes at a rate of 10 per day since she began in 2007. She quit smoking in January 2023. She currently consumes approximately four alcoholic drinks per week. Her family history is significant for COPD in her father, who was diagnosed at the age of 66.\n\n**ASSESSMENT/PLAN:**\n- **Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus:** Ms. Miller was diagnosed with Type 2 diabetes at the age of 30. Her diabetes is currently controlled, as evidenced by her A1c levels remaining consistently within the target range. She is taking metformin 500 mg daily, which has effectively maintained her blood glucose levels. Given her compliance with medication and lifestyle modifications, the current is to continue the current treatment regimen. Further, it is recommended that Ms. Miller maintains regular follow-ups to monitor her condition and address any potential complications.\n\n**SUMMARY:**\nMs. Breanna Miller has been managing her Type 2 diabetes effectively with a stable A1c level of 3.2%. Her adherence to her medication regimen of metformin 500 mg daily and her commitment to regular monitoring have contributed to her well-controlled diabetes. She has a positive outlook on managing her condition, and there are no immediate concerns regarding her overall health status.\n\n**Dr. Gerald Gross** \nEdwards-Burns Clinic \nSpecialty: Endocrinology\n"
